A||||6||sqoach||Clattering noise when I turn||||1||
Z||000000||sqoach||08-02-2001||12:25 PM||||Every time I take a right turn, there's this crappy sounding clatter. It doesn't do it when I turn left. Anyone have any ideas?<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <P>84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-02-2001||12:30 PM||||Does it happen at high speed, low speed, any speed?<BR>Does it happen in any right turn or full lock only?<BR>Does it seem to come from the right or left?<BR>Does the steering wheel shake/rattle?<BR>Is there any play in the road wheels with the front end jacked up?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||08-02-2001||12:30 PM||||When you turn apply the breaks and see if it stops.<BR>I had a chatter in the left rear of the car that turned out to be the break pads. <BR>The gap was over a 1/4 of an inch and when I turned the pad would rattle against the rotor.<BR>New calipers and pads = no more noise.<BR>You may just need new pads.<P><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://web.tampabay.rr.com/jakedragon" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/logo.gif"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||08-02-2001||12:41 PM||||Um...let's see. I can hear it when I turn my wheel 1/4 turn to the right or more. So every time I take a right turn. I don't take corners at high speeds. The sounds seems to be coming from the right side. And I don't notice the steering wheel shaking or anything when I turn. But it does when I'm driving on the highway. I think I might need an alignment. Also, my brakes kinda suck. They don't stop real good, and they make this annoying "metal on metat" sound, but the rear pads are new, and the fronts are still pretty good.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||08-02-2001||02:06 PM||||If your e-brake works, try this.<BR>Set the e-brake, depress brake pedal and hold down, release e-brake and release pedal. Repeat.<BR>If that doesn't work do all steps again but don't release pedal until you re-set the e-brake  and then push it down and continue.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||08-02-2001||05:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>I don't take corners at high speeds.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So why own a Fiero?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Jack up the right side and test the wheel for bad bearings. Put one hand on the top and one on the bottom of the wheel and rock it back and forth. Lock the steering wheel and do the same thing from side to side.||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-02-2001||06:28 PM||||I had an 84 SE that did the same thing. I knew what it was, but was too lazy to fix it, so it got my attention. I was driving down my street at 20mph and I heard a load "THUNK," and then a nasty scraping sound. I got out to realize the lower ball joint let go and the lower A-arm was resting rather tenaciously against the wheel. My car also did this on r/h turns, although no relevant coincidence. The reason it did it on right turns was that the right front wheel was unloaded, as I do take turns fast. What you need to do, either yourself or at a shop, (preferably yourself) is to jack the front end and collapse the springs. You can then move the various front end parts around unabated. You can check the relationship between the inner A-arm bushings and the bolts that run through them. And you can check the upper and lower ball joints located at the outer control arms. Don't put them off if they're bad. The reason I say do it yourself is because a shop might try to sell you the job even if you don't need it. But if you are unable then you'll have to take it there. Oh, check the sway bar attachments also.||||reg||1
